## Experiment 7: Travelling Salesman Problem

**Q1. What is the Travelling Salesman Problem?**

Ans: An optimization problem in which a salesman must visit every city exactly once and return to the starting city with minimum travelling cost.

**Q2. Which algorithm technique is used in this experiment?**

Ans: Dynamic Programming.

**Q3. What is Dynamic Programming?**

Ans: An algorithm design technique that solves overlapping subproblems and stores their results to avoid repeated calculations.

**Q4. What are the two important properties of Dynamic Programming?**

Ans: Optimal substructure and overlapping subproblems.

**Q5. What is optimal substructure?**

Ans: A property in which an optimal solution can be constructed using optimal solutions of smaller subproblems.

**Q6. What are overlapping subproblems?**

Ans: Subproblems that occur repeatedly during the computation.

**Q7. What is memoization?**

Ans: A top-down Dynamic Programming technique that stores previously calculated results.

**Q8. What is tabulation?**

Ans: A bottom-up Dynamic Programming technique that calculates and stores subproblem results iteratively.

**Q9. Which Dynamic Programming approach is used in our program?**

Ans: Top-down Dynamic Programming using memoization.

**Q10. What is bitmasking?**

Ans: A technique that uses binary bits to represent the state of a collection of elements.

**Q11. Why is bitmasking used in TSP?**

Ans: To efficiently represent the set of visited cities.

**Q12. What does a set bit represent?**

Ans: A visited city.

**Q13. What does an unset bit represent?**

Ans: An unvisited city.

**Q14. What does dp[mask][pos] represent?**

Ans: The minimum remaining travelling cost when the salesman is at city pos and the cities represented by mask have already been visited.

**Q15. What is the initial bitmask in our program?**

Ans: 1, indicating that city 0 has been visited.

**Q16. What is the final bitmask?**

Ans: (1 << n) - 1, indicating that all cities have been visited.

**Q17. What is the base condition of the TSP function?**

Ans: When all cities have been visited, return the cost of travelling back to the starting city.

**Q18. How do we check whether a city has been visited?**

Ans: Using the bitwise AND operation between the mask and (1 << city).

**Q19. How do we mark a city as visited?**

Ans: Using the bitwise OR operation: mask | (1 << city).

**Q20. Why is the DP table initialized to -1?**

Ans: To indicate that the corresponding subproblem has not yet been calculated.

**Q21. What is the purpose of the nextCity table?**

Ans: To store the next city selected in an optimal route.

**Q22. What is the time complexity of brute force TSP?**

Ans: O(N!).

**Q23. What is the time complexity of TSP using Dynamic Programming?**

Ans: O(N^2 * 2^N).

**Q24. What is the space complexity of TSP using Dynamic Programming?**

Ans: O(N * 2^N).

**Q25. What is the Held-Karp Algorithm?**

Ans: An exact Dynamic Programming algorithm for solving the Travelling Salesman Problem using subsets of cities.

**Q26. Is TSP an optimization problem?**

Ans: Yes. Its objective is to minimize the total travelling cost.

**Q27. Is the Travelling Salesman optimization problem NP-hard?**

Ans: Yes.

**Q28. Is the decision version of TSP NP-complete?**

Ans: Yes.

**Q29. What is a Hamiltonian Cycle?**

Ans: A cycle that visits every vertex exactly once before returning to the starting vertex.

**Q30. What is the difference between TSP and the Hamiltonian Cycle problem?**

Ans: The Hamiltonian Cycle problem checks whether such a cycle exists, whereas TSP finds a minimum-cost Hamiltonian Cycle.

**Q31. What is symmetric TSP?**

Ans: A TSP in which the travelling cost from city A to city B equals the cost from city B to city A.

**Q32. What is asymmetric TSP?**

Ans: A TSP in which travelling costs between cities may differ depending on the direction.

**Q33. Can our program solve asymmetric TSP?**

Ans: Yes, because it uses the supplied directional travelling costs.

**Q34. Does the greedy approach always find the optimal TSP solution?**

Ans: No.

**Q35. Does Dynamic Programming guarantee the optimal TSP solution?**

Ans: Yes, for the problem represented by the complete cost matrix.

**Q36. What is the nearest-neighbor algorithm?**

Ans: A heuristic that repeatedly visits the nearest unvisited city.

**Q37. Why is Dynamic Programming better than brute force for TSP?**

Ans: It avoids recalculating identical subproblems and reduces factorial-scale search to exponential-scale computation.

**Q38. Can multiple optimal routes exist in TSP?**

Ans: Yes.

**Q39. What is the minimum travelling cost in our first test case?**

Ans: 80.

**Q40. What is the optimal route in our first test case?**

Ans: 0 -> 1 -> 3 -> 2 -> 0.

**Q41. Why does the salesman return to the starting city?**

Ans: Returning to the starting city is a requirement of the classical Travelling Salesman Problem.

**Q42. What is the difference between TSP and the shortest path problem?**

Ans: TSP visits every city and returns to the starting city, whereas the shortest path problem finds a minimum-cost path between specified vertices.

**Q43. What is the main disadvantage of Dynamic Programming for TSP?**

Ans: It requires exponential time and memory as the number of cities increases.

**Q44. Why is the number of cities restricted to 15 in our program?**

Ans: To limit exponential memory consumption and execution time.

**Q45. What are the real-world applications of TSP?**

Ans: Delivery route optimization, logistics, transportation, robot navigation and manufacturing.

**Q46. What is the difference between Dynamic Programming and Divide and Conquer?**

Ans: Dynamic Programming stores solutions to overlapping subproblems, whereas Divide and Conquer generally solves independent subproblems.

**Q47. Can Branch and Bound solve TSP?**

Ans: Yes. It uses bounds to eliminate routes that cannot improve the best known solution.

**Q48. What is the purpose of the travelling cost matrix?**

Ans: To store the travelling cost between every pair of cities.

**Q49. How many possible tours exist for N cities with a fixed starting city?**

Ans: (N - 1)! directed visiting orders.

**Q50. What is the objective function of TSP?**

Ans: Minimizing the sum of travelling costs along a complete tour.
